//! AES-GCM authenticated encryption (AEAD).
//!
//! This file implements AES in Galois/Counter Mode as specified by NIST
//! SP 800-38D. GCM is an AEAD scheme: a single pass produces both confidentiality
//! (the ciphertext) and integrity (a 16-byte authentication tag) over the plaintext
//! plus any additional authenticated data (AAD) that travels in the clear. It is
//! the workhorse cipher behind TLS 1.2/1.3 record protection, and it is the reason
//! a server on this box prefers AES-GCM over ChaCha20-Poly1305 whenever the CPU
//! carries the AES-NI instructions (see the crypto-bench note in project memory).
//!
//! GCM works by running AES in counter mode (CTR) to make the keystream, and then
//! authenticating with GHASH, a keyed hash over GF(2^128). The universal-hash key
//! `H` is `AES_K(0^128)`, the encryption of an all-zero block. The tag is
//! `GHASH(H, AAD || C || lengths) XOR AES_K(J0)`, where `J0` is a per-message counter
//! seed derived from the IV. All of that machinery lives in [`GcmContext.seal`]
//! and [`GcmContext.open`], with [`ghash.Ghash`] supplying the field arithmetic and
//! [`aes.Aes`] the block cipher.
//!
//! Two implementations coexist behind one API. When the runtime probe
//! [`nova_cpu_has_aes`] reports AES-NI and the carry-less multiply (PCLMULQDQ) are
//! present, [`GcmContext`] delegates the whole seal/open to the assembly kernels in
//! [`aesgcmhw`], which fuse CTR and GHASH over several blocks at once. Otherwise it
//! falls back to the portable software path in this file, built from [`gctr`],
//! [`computeJ0`], [`computeS`] and friends. The two paths are byte-for-byte
//! equivalent; only the speed differs.
//!
//! Two design points that the caller must respect:
//!
//!   1. The tag comparison in [`GcmContext.open`] is CONSTANT TIME. It accumulates
//!      the XOR difference of all 16 bytes into `diff` and only tests `diff == 0`
//!      at the end, so a forged tag cannot be probed byte by byte through timing.
//!      The plaintext is written out ONLY after the tag verifies, so a caller never
//!      sees unauthenticated data.
//!
//!   2. Every function works on raw `ptr` buffers the caller owns and sizes. There
//!      is no bounds checking: the output buffers must be large enough (ciphertext
//!      the same length as plaintext, tag [`GCM_TAG_LEN`] bytes), and a nonce must
//!      NEVER be reused with the same key. GCM catastrophically loses both secrecy
//!      of the authentication key and integrity if a (key, IV) pair repeats.
//!
//! The two module-level [`seal`] and [`open`] helpers are the one-shot convenience
//! API: they build a [`GcmContext`], run the operation, and tear it down. For
//! repeated operations under one key, build a [`GcmContext`] once (the H and the
//! hardware key schedule are computed at construction) and reuse it.

import bytes;
import crypto.cipher.aes;
import crypto.mac.ghash;
import crypto.aead.aesgcmhw;
import mem.allocator;

/// Runtime probe for the CPU's AES and carry-less-multiply instruction support.
///
/// Implemented in the C runtime (via `cpuid` on x86, feature registers on ARM). It
/// returns `1` when AES-NI plus PCLMULQDQ (or the ARM crypto extensions) are usable,
/// and `0` otherwise. [`GcmContext.create`] reads this once and latches the answer in
/// [`GcmContext.hwAes`] to pick the hardware kernels in [`aesgcmhw`] over the software
/// fallback in this file.
extern("c") fn nova_cpu_has_aes(): int;

/// Length of a GCM authentication tag in bytes: 16, i.e. the full 128-bit tag.
///
/// GCM permits truncated tags, but this implementation always produces and expects
/// the full-length tag. Callers should size the `tagOut` and `tag` buffers passed to
/// [`GcmContext.seal`] and [`GcmContext.open`] to exactly this many bytes.
pub const GCM_TAG_LEN: int = 16;

/// Increments the rightmost 32 bits of a 16-byte counter block in place, big-endian.
///
/// This is GCM's `inc32` function. Only bytes 12..15 form the counter; bytes 0..11
/// (the invocation/nonce field) are left untouched. The add ripples the carry from
/// byte 15 down to byte 12 and then stops, so the counter wraps modulo 2^32 within
/// its own 4-byte field rather than overflowing into the nonce. Used to step the CTR
/// keystream block by block in [`gctr`], and to turn `J0` into the first data counter
/// `J0 + 1` in [`GcmContext.seal`] and [`GcmContext.open`].
fn inc32(ctr: ptr): void {
    let i = 15;
    let carry = 1;
    while (i >= 12) {
        let v = bytes.read_byte(ctr, i) + carry;
        bytes.write_byte(ctr, i, v & 0xff);
        carry = v >> 8;
        i = i - 1;
    }
}

/// Writes a 64-bit value as 8 big-endian bytes into `buf` starting at `off`.
///
/// GCM's length-encoding and GHASH block layout are big-endian (network order), so
/// this stores the most significant byte first. It is used to lay down the bit-length
/// fields that GHASH folds in last: the IV bit length in [`computeJ0`] and the AAD and
/// ciphertext bit lengths in [`computeS`]. The value is a `long` because a bit count
/// can exceed 32 bits.
fn putLen64(buf: ptr, off: int, bits: long): void {
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 8) {
        bytes.write_byte(buf, off + i, ((bits >> ((7 - i) * 8)) & 0xff) as int);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// AES in counter mode: the GCTR function of SP 800-38D.
///
/// Encrypts (or decrypts, the operation is symmetric) `len` bytes from `dataIn` into
/// `out` by XORing them with the AES keystream. Starting from the initial counter
/// block `icb`, it encrypts each counter to a 16-byte keystream block, XORs it over
/// the next slice of input, then advances the counter with [`inc32`]. The final
/// partial block uses only its first `n` keystream bytes, so the ciphertext is exactly
/// as long as the plaintext (GCM is length-preserving, no padding).
///
/// `icb` is consumed as a starting value and left unmodified by the caller's copy: this
/// function copies it into a private `ctr` buffer first, so the caller's `icb` is not
/// mutated. `dataIn` and `out` may be distinct buffers of length `len`. Because encrypt
/// and decrypt are the same XOR, [`GcmContext.seal`] and [`GcmContext.open`] both call
/// this with the counter seeded at `J0 + 1`.
fn gctr(ctx: aes.Aes, icb: ptr, dataIn: ptr, len: int, out: ptr): void {
    let ctr = bytes.alloc(16);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 16) { bytes.write_byte(ctr, i, bytes.read_byte(icb, i)); i = i + 1; }
    let ks = bytes.alloc(16);
    let done = 0;
    while (done < len) {
        ctx.encryptBlock(ctr, ks);
        let n = if (len - done < 16) (len - done) else 16;
        let k = 0;
        while (k < n) {
            bytes.write_byte(out, done + k, bytes.read_byte(dataIn, done + k) ^ bytes.read_byte(ks, k));
            k = k + 1;
        }
        inc32(ctr);
        done = done + n;
    }
    bytes.free(ctr);
    bytes.free(ks);
}

/// Derives the GHASH subkey `H` by encrypting an all-zero block under the AES key.
///
/// GHASH is keyed by `H = AES_K(0^128)`, so this fills a scratch 16-byte block with
/// zeros and encrypts it into `h`. It is called once at construction time from
/// [`GcmContext.create`]; the resulting `H` is then handed to [`ghash.Ghash.create`]
/// and reused for every message under that key.
fn computeH(ctx: aes.Aes, h: ptr): void {
    let zero = bytes.alloc(16);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 16) { bytes.write_byte(zero, i, 0); i = i + 1; }
    ctx.encryptBlock(zero, h);
    bytes.free(zero);
}

/// Computes the pre-counter block `J0` from the IV, per SP 800-38D.
///
/// `J0` is the seed the counter mode starts from. GCM specifies two cases and this
/// function implements both:
///
///   1. The common case of a 96-bit (12-byte) IV: `J0` is the IV followed by the
///      32-bit big-endian integer 1, i.e. `IV || 0x00000001`. This is the fast path
///      TLS uses.
///   2. Any other IV length: `J0 = GHASH(H, IV || zero-pad || len64(IV))`. Here the
///      IV is fed through GHASH, then a final block carrying the IV bit length in its
///      low 8 bytes (high 8 bytes zero) is folded in, and the digest becomes `J0`.
///
/// The `g` GHASH state is only used in the second branch; it is reset first so any
/// prior message state is discarded. The 16-byte result is written to `j0`.
fn computeJ0(g: ghash.Ghash, iv: ptr, ivLen: int, j0: ptr): void {
    if (ivLen == 12) {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < 12) { bytes.write_byte(j0, i, bytes.read_byte(iv, i)); i = i + 1; }
        bytes.write_byte(j0, 12, 0);
        bytes.write_byte(j0, 13, 0);
        bytes.write_byte(j0, 14, 0);
        bytes.write_byte(j0, 15, 1);
    } else {
        g.reset();
        g.update(iv, ivLen);
        let lb = bytes.alloc(16);
        let k = 0;
        while (k < 8) { bytes.write_byte(lb, k, 0); k = k + 1; }
        putLen64(lb, 8, (ivLen as long) * 8);
        g.block(lb, 0);
        g.final(j0);
        bytes.free(lb);
    }
}

/// Computes the GHASH digest `S` over the AAD and ciphertext, the tag's pre-image.
///
/// This is the authentication core: `S = GHASH(H, AAD || C || len64(AAD) || len64(C))`.
/// It resets `g`, streams the AAD and then the ciphertext through it (GHASH pads each
/// to a block boundary internally), and finally folds one more block carrying the AAD
/// bit length in bytes 0..7 and the ciphertext bit length in bytes 8..15. The 16-byte
/// digest is written to `s`. The caller then XORs `S` with `AES_K(J0)` to get the tag
/// on seal, or compares against the supplied tag on open. Note it hashes the
/// CIPHERTEXT, not the plaintext, which is why open can verify before decrypting.
fn computeS(g: ghash.Ghash, aad: ptr, aadLen: int, ct: ptr, ctLen: int, s: ptr): void {
    g.reset();
    g.update(aad, aadLen);
    g.update(ct, ctLen);
    let lens = bytes.alloc(16);
    putLen64(lens, 0, (aadLen as long) * 8);
    putLen64(lens, 8, (ctLen as long) * 8);
    g.block(lens, 0);
    g.final(s);
    bytes.free(lens);
}

/// A reusable AES-GCM cipher context bound to one key.
///
/// Construction is where the per-key precomputation happens: the AES key schedule,
/// the GHASH subkey `H` (see [`computeH`]), and, on capable CPUs, the hardware key
/// schedule and precomputed `H` powers. Once built, a single context can [`seal`]
/// and [`open`] many messages, each with its own IV, without redoing that work.
///
/// A context holds two parallel sets of state and picks between them by [`hwAes`]:
/// the software path uses [`ctx`], [`h`] and [`g`]; the hardware path in [`aesgcmhw`]
/// uses the `hw*` fields. Only one set is exercised per operation. The context owns
/// several heap buffers, so the caller MUST call [`delete`] to free them.
pub struct GcmContext {
    /// The AES block-cipher context (expanded key schedule) for the software path.
    pub ctx: aes.Aes,
    /// The 16-byte GHASH subkey `H = AES_K(0^128)`, owned by this context.
    pub h: ptr,
    /// The GHASH universal-hash state, keyed by [`h`], used by the software path.
    pub g: ghash.Ghash,

    /// True when the CPU offers AES-NI and PCLMULQDQ, latched from [`nova_cpu_has_aes`]
    /// at construction. When true, [`seal`] and [`open`] delegate wholesale to
    /// [`aesgcmhw`] and the software fields go unused; when false, the reverse.
    pub hwAes: bool,
    /// Number of AES rounds for the hardware kernels (10/12/14 for 128/192/256-bit
    /// keys). Set by [`aesgcmhw.setup`]; 0 when [`hwAes`] is false.
    pub hwNr: int,
    /// Hardware round-key schedule buffer (240 bytes, enough for AES-256), or 0.
    pub hwRk: ptr,
    /// The GHASH subkey `H` in the byte order the hardware kernel expects, or 0.
    pub hwHStd: ptr,
    /// Bit-reversal or byte-reversal scratch table used by the hardware GHASH, or 0.
    pub hwBrev: ptr,
    /// Precomputed powers of `H` that let the hardware kernel fold several blocks per
    /// carry-less multiply, or 0.
    pub hwHpows: ptr,

    /// Builds a context for `key` (of `keyLen` bytes: 16, 24, or 32) and precomputes
    /// everything reusable across messages.
    ///
    /// It always sets up the software path (AES schedule, `H` via [`computeH`], and the
    /// GHASH state). It then probes [`nova_cpu_has_aes`]; if the CPU is capable, it also
    /// allocates the hardware buffers and calls [`aesgcmhw.setup`] to fill them, latching
    /// [`hwAes`] true. If not, the `hw*` pointer fields are set to 0 so [`delete`] knows
    /// there is nothing to free there. The returned context owns heap memory and must be
    /// released with [`delete`].
    pub fn create(key: ptr, keyLen: int): GcmContext {
        let c = bytes.new_with_allocator<GcmContext>(allocator.globalAllocator());
        c.ctx = aes.Aes.create(key, keyLen);
        c.h = bytes.alloc(16);
        computeH(c.ctx, c.h);
        c.g = ghash.Ghash.create(c.h);
        c.hwAes = nova_cpu_has_aes() == 1;
        if (c.hwAes) {
            c.hwRk = bytes.alloc(240);
            c.hwHStd = bytes.alloc(16);
            c.hwBrev = bytes.alloc(256);
            c.hwHpows = bytes.alloc(64);
            c.hwNr = aesgcmhw.setup(key, keyLen, c.hwRk, c.hwHStd, c.hwBrev, c.hwHpows);
        } else {
            c.hwNr = 0; c.hwRk = 0; c.hwHStd = 0; c.hwBrev = 0; c.hwHpows = 0;
        }
        return c;
    }

    /// Encrypts and authenticates: writes `ptLen` ciphertext bytes to `ctOut` and a
    /// [`GCM_TAG_LEN`]-byte tag to `tagOut`.
    ///
    /// The tag authenticates both the AAD (`aad`, `aadLen`, which stays in the clear)
    /// and the ciphertext. On a hardware-capable CPU this is one call into
    /// [`aesgcmhw.sealWith`]. The software path (a) derives `J0` from the IV with
    /// [`computeJ0`], (b) runs CTR from `J0 + 1` over the plaintext with [`gctr`],
    /// (c) computes `S` over AAD and ciphertext with [`computeS`], and (d) forms the
    /// tag as `S XOR AES_K(J0)`.
    ///
    /// `ctOut` must have room for `ptLen` bytes and may alias `pt` for in-place
    /// encryption; `tagOut` must have room for [`GCM_TAG_LEN`] bytes. The IV must be
    /// unique per message under this key: reusing an IV breaks GCM's security entirely.
    pub fn seal(self: GcmContext, iv: ptr, ivLen: int, aad: ptr, aadLen: int,
                pt: ptr, ptLen: int, ctOut: ptr, tagOut: ptr): void {
        if (self.hwAes) {
            aesgcmhw.sealWith(self.hwRk, self.hwNr, self.hwHStd, self.hwBrev, self.hwHpows,
                              iv, ivLen, aad, aadLen, pt, ptLen, ctOut, tagOut);
            return;
        }
        let j0 = bytes.alloc(16);
        computeJ0(self.g, iv, ivLen, j0);

        let icb = bytes.alloc(16);
        let i = 0;
        while (i < 16) { bytes.write_byte(icb, i, bytes.read_byte(j0, i)); i = i + 1; }
        inc32(icb);
        gctr(self.ctx, icb, pt, ptLen, ctOut);

        let s = bytes.alloc(16);
        computeS(self.g, aad, aadLen, ctOut, ptLen, s);
        let ej0 = bytes.alloc(16);
        self.ctx.encryptBlock(j0, ej0);
        i = 0;
        while (i < 16) { bytes.write_byte(tagOut, i, bytes.read_byte(s, i) ^ bytes.read_byte(ej0, i)); i = i + 1; }

        bytes.free(j0); bytes.free(icb); bytes.free(s); bytes.free(ej0);
    }

    /// Verifies the tag and, only if it is valid, decrypts: returns whether the message
    /// was authentic.
    ///
    /// This is the inverse of [`seal`]. It recomputes the expected tag from the IV, AAD,
    /// and supplied ciphertext, and compares it against `tag`. Two safety properties are
    /// deliberate here. First, the comparison is CONSTANT TIME: it ORs the XOR of all 16
    /// byte differences into `diff` and tests `diff == 0` once, so a forgery cannot be
    /// tuned byte by byte through timing. Second, the plaintext is written to `ptOut`
    /// (via [`gctr`] from `J0 + 1`) ONLY after the tag verifies, so a caller never
    /// observes unauthenticated plaintext. On a mismatch it returns `false` and leaves
    /// `ptOut` untouched.
    ///
    /// `ptOut` must have room for `ctLen` bytes and may alias `ct`. On a hardware-capable
    /// CPU the whole operation is delegated to [`aesgcmhw.openWith`].
    pub fn open(self: GcmContext, iv: ptr, ivLen: int, aad: ptr, aadLen: int,
                ct: ptr, ctLen: int, tag: ptr, ptOut: ptr): bool {
        if (self.hwAes) {
            return aesgcmhw.openWith(self.hwRk, self.hwNr, self.hwHStd, self.hwBrev,
                                     iv, ivLen, aad, aadLen, ct, ctLen, tag, ptOut);
        }
        let j0 = bytes.alloc(16);
        computeJ0(self.g, iv, ivLen, j0);

        let s = bytes.alloc(16);
        computeS(self.g, aad, aadLen, ct, ctLen, s);
        let ej0 = bytes.alloc(16);
        self.ctx.encryptBlock(j0, ej0);

        let diff = 0;
        let i = 0;
        while (i < 16) {
            diff = diff | ((bytes.read_byte(s, i) ^ bytes.read_byte(ej0, i)) ^ bytes.read_byte(tag, i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        let ok = diff == 0;
        if (ok) {
            let icb = bytes.alloc(16);
            i = 0;
            while (i < 16) { bytes.write_byte(icb, i, bytes.read_byte(j0, i)); i = i + 1; }
            inc32(icb);
            gctr(self.ctx, icb, ct, ctLen, ptOut);
            bytes.free(icb);
        }

        bytes.free(j0); bytes.free(s); bytes.free(ej0);
        return ok;
    }

    /// Frees every heap buffer the context owns and tears down its sub-contexts.
    ///
    /// It deletes the AES schedule [`ctx`] and GHASH state [`g`], frees the subkey [`h`],
    /// and frees whichever `hw*` buffers were allocated by [`create`]. Each pointer is
    /// guarded by a non-zero check and then zeroed, so calling [`delete`] more than once
    /// is safe (the second call finds every pointer already 0 and does nothing). Must be
    /// called exactly once when the context is no longer needed.
    pub fn delete(self: GcmContext): void {
        self.ctx.delete();
        self.g.delete();
        if (self.h != 0) { bytes.free(self.h); self.h = 0; }
        if (self.hwRk != 0) { bytes.free(self.hwRk); self.hwRk = 0; }
        if (self.hwHStd != 0) { bytes.free(self.hwHStd); self.hwHStd = 0; }
        if (self.hwBrev != 0) { bytes.free(self.hwBrev); self.hwBrev = 0; }
        if (self.hwHpows != 0) { bytes.free(self.hwHpows); self.hwHpows = 0; }
    }
}

/// One-shot AES-GCM encryption: build a context, seal, and tear it down.
///
/// A convenience wrapper over [`GcmContext.create`], [`GcmContext.seal`], and
/// [`GcmContext.delete`] for callers who encrypt a single message. It redoes the
/// per-key precomputation every call, so for many messages under one key build a
/// [`GcmContext`] once and reuse it instead. Buffer sizing and the no-IV-reuse rule
/// are exactly as documented on [`GcmContext.seal`].
pub fn seal(key: ptr, keyLen: int, iv: ptr, ivLen: int, aad: ptr, aadLen: int,
            pt: ptr, ptLen: int, ctOut: ptr, tagOut: ptr): void {
    let c = GcmContext.create(key, keyLen);
    c.seal(iv, ivLen, aad, aadLen, pt, ptLen, ctOut, tagOut);
    c.delete();
}

/// One-shot AES-GCM decryption and verification: build a context, open, and tear it down.
///
/// A convenience wrapper over [`GcmContext.create`], [`GcmContext.open`], and
/// [`GcmContext.delete`]. It returns `true` and fills `ptOut` when the tag is authentic,
/// or `false` (leaving `ptOut` untouched) when verification fails, with the same
/// constant-time and verify-before-decrypt guarantees as [`GcmContext.open`]. Prefer a
/// reused [`GcmContext`] when opening many messages under one key.
pub fn open(key: ptr, keyLen: int, iv: ptr, ivLen: int, aad: ptr, aadLen: int,
            ct: ptr, ctLen: int, tag: ptr, ptOut: ptr): bool {
    let c = GcmContext.create(key, keyLen);
    let ok = c.open(iv, ivLen, aad, aadLen, ct, ctLen, tag, ptOut);
    c.delete();
    return ok;
}
